1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,281
This is your branch after a
day of real work. Six commits.

2
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:13,091
One is called "wip". One is a debug
print. One has a typo in its subject.

3
00:00:13,131 --> 00:00:16,846
A test and the fix for that
test are three commits apart.

4
00:00:16,886 --> 00:00:20,997
Nothing to be ashamed of: every
working branch looks like this.

5
00:00:21,337 --> 00:00:26,254
Next month a regression shows up in the
F1 metric. Someone runs git bisect, the

6
00:00:26,294 --> 00:00:31,086
binary search over commits, across this
history. Bisect lands on "wip", which

7
00:00:31,126 --> 00:00:35,918
doesn't build. It lands on "debug print",
which changes nothing that matters.

8
00:00:35,958 --> 00:00:41,063
And when they finally find the commit, they
want to revert it, and it contains two

9
00:00:41,103 --> 00:00:42,318
unrelated decisions.

10
00:00:42,658 --> 00:00:47,199
A tidy history isn't about beauty. It's
about whether those two tools work.

11
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:52,145
Keep this list of six in mind. In the demo
it becomes three commits, one decision

12
00:00:52,185 --> 00:00:52,450
each.

13
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:58,022
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:58,062 --> 00:01:01,855
A rebase copies commits onto a
base and then moves the branch.

15
00:01:01,895 --> 00:01:06,492
In the last three videos, it ran a list
of instructions that you never saw.

16
00:01:06,532 --> 00:01:11,438
Interactive rebase shows you the list
before executing it, and lets you edit it.

17
00:01:11,778 --> 00:01:16,042
That's the only new idea today, and
everything else follows from it.

18
00:01:16,082 --> 00:01:20,915
The list is a program. One instruction
per line, executed from top to bottom.

19
00:01:20,955 --> 00:01:23,700
You may edit, reorder,
extend or shorten it.

20
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:28,971
You'll use eight instructions. Five rearrange
history: reword, fixup, squash, drop,

21
00:01:29,011 --> 00:01:33,583
and moving a line. One stops inside
history so that you can change or split a

22
00:01:33,623 --> 00:01:38,135
commit: edit. One runs a command after
a commit and stops if it fails: exec.

23
00:01:38,175 --> 00:01:40,411
And one stops and does nothing: break.

24
00:01:40,751 --> 00:01:44,288
One note on the transcripts,
said once for the whole module.

25
00:01:44,328 --> 00:01:49,057
An interactive rebase opens an editor. In
these replays a small script plays the

26
00:01:49,097 --> 00:01:53,946
person at the keyboard. It prints the list
as Git opened it, applies the edit, and

27
00:01:53,986 --> 00:01:58,775
prints the list as saved. Those two blocks
stand for what you would see and do in

28
00:01:58,815 --> 00:01:59,729
your own editor.

29
00:02:01,569 --> 00:02:02,981
After this video you can:

30
00:02:03,321 --> 00:02:06,606
Read and edit a todo list and
predict the resulting history.

31
00:02:06,946 --> 00:02:10,477
Reword, squash, fix up,
drop and reorder commits.

32
00:02:10,817 --> 00:02:13,388
Stop at a commit with edit
and split it into two.

33
00:02:13,728 --> 00:02:17,639
Run a test after every commit with
exec, and recover when it fails.

34
00:02:17,979 --> 00:02:22,076
Explain why ORIG_HEAD may not point
at the pre-rebase tip afterwards.

35
00:02:23,916 --> 00:02:29,014
In one sentence: git rebase -i <base> 🟡
CAUTION shows you the to-do list before

36
00:02:29,054 --> 00:02:34,476
executing it, and the list is a program:
one instruction per line, executed from top

37
00:02:34,516 --> 00:02:38,183
to bottom, that you may edit,
reorder, extend or shorten.

38
00:02:38,523 --> 00:02:41,845
<base> is the last commit
you want to keep as it is.

39
00:02:41,885 --> 00:02:45,272
git rebase -i main offers
every commit of the branch.

40
00:02:45,312 --> 00:02:48,053
git rebase -i HEAD~3
offers the last three.

41
00:02:51,393 --> 00:02:56,112
Read the last row twice. A deleted line
is a dropped commit, and Git doesn't ask.

42
00:02:56,452 --> 00:03:01,620
Inside .git: nothing new. It's the
machinery from two videos ago: the state

43
00:03:01,660 --> 00:03:06,342
directory, the detached HEAD, the
branch that moves only at the end.

44
00:03:06,382 --> 00:03:12,035
The one difference: the instruction file
is handed to your editor before execution

45
00:03:12,075 --> 00:03:17,799
starts. The editor is sequence.editor or
GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR if set, otherwise your

46
00:03:17,839 --> 00:03:19,812
normal commit message editor.

47
00:03:20,152 --> 00:03:25,054
Because it's the same machinery, the same
fact holds: while the rebase is stopped,

48
00:03:25,094 --> 00:03:29,996
at an edit, at a failed exec, at a break,
you're on a detached HEAD and the branch

49
00:03:30,036 --> 00:03:34,998
hasn't moved. On screen, a rebase has stopped
at an edit on the middle commit: HEAD

50
00:03:35,038 --> 00:03:39,819
is there, and the branch label isn't.
Commits you make during a stop are made on

51
00:03:39,859 --> 00:03:40,964
that detached HEAD.

52
00:03:41,304 --> 00:03:45,925
And one consequence for undo. Commands
you run during a stop can overwrite

53
00:03:45,965 --> 00:03:50,712
ORIG_HEAD. git reset writes it. So after
an interactive rebase with a split,

54
00:03:50,752 --> 00:03:54,114
ORIG_HEAD may not name the
tip from before the rebase.

55
00:03:54,154 --> 00:03:56,003
The reflog of the branch does.

56
00:03:56,343 --> 00:04:00,149
When not to use it: on a branch
other people have based work on.

57
00:04:00,189 --> 00:04:01,651
All of this is rewriting.

58
00:04:03,491 --> 00:04:08,412
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
an edit decision list in film editing.

59
00:04:08,452 --> 00:04:13,687
A list of takes with an instruction for
each: keep, cut, join with the previous one,

60
00:04:13,727 --> 00:04:17,958
retitle. The footage isn't altered.
A new reel is assembled from it.

61
00:04:18,298 --> 00:04:23,043
Where it breaks: commits depend on each
other. Move a take and the film still plays.

62
00:04:23,083 --> 00:04:27,429
Move a commit in front of the commit it
builds on and the replay stops with a

63
00:04:27,469 --> 00:04:27,942
conflict.

64
00:04:28,282 --> 00:04:32,952
Two reading habits follow. First, the list
runs oldest first, the opposite of git

65
00:04:32,992 --> 00:04:37,720
log. Top of the list is the bottom of the
log. Second, squash and fixup act on the

66
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:42,372
line above. So to combine two commits that
aren't neighbours, you first move one

67
00:04:42,412 --> 00:04:44,174
line, and then change its verb.

68
00:04:44,514 --> 00:04:49,516
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. The
list says pick A, pick B, pick C, and you

69
00:04:49,556 --> 00:04:53,950
want C melded into A. Write the list
you would save, and say it out loud.

70
00:04:58,790 --> 00:05:02,507
Pick A, fixup C, pick B: one
line moved, one verb changed.

71
00:05:02,547 --> 00:05:05,552
The verb goes on the
commit that's melded away.

72
00:05:07,392 --> 00:05:11,664
This is the map for the demo. Left:
the todo list, oldest at the top.

73
00:05:11,704 --> 00:05:16,914
Right: the history each edit produces. A
prime marks a new ID, and every commit from

74
00:05:16,954 --> 00:05:19,351
the first changed line onward gets one.

75
00:05:21,191 --> 00:05:25,861
That's the branch from the hook. First,
the list exactly as Git writes it.

76
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:31,144
Using cat as the editor prints the file and
saves it unchanged, so this rebase does

77
00:05:31,184 --> 00:05:31,653
nothing.

78
00:05:31,993 --> 00:05:36,788
Six pick lines, oldest first. Below them,
the comment block, which is the complete

79
00:05:36,828 --> 00:05:40,384
reference. You never need to
look the verbs up anywhere else.

80
00:05:40,424 --> 00:05:45,042
And in it, one line in capitals: "If you
remove a line here THAT COMMIT WILL BE

81
00:05:45,082 --> 00:05:45,396
LOST."

82
00:05:45,736 --> 00:05:51,005
Now six rebases, each with one edit, so
that each instruction can be seen alone.

83
00:05:51,045 --> 00:05:55,252
In practice you make all edits in
one pass, and that is Lab 9.1.

84
00:05:55,592 --> 00:05:58,787
reword, on the commit with
the typo in its subject.

85
00:05:58,827 --> 00:06:03,544
Predict: how many of the six commits
get a new ID? Say the number out loud.

86
00:06:03,584 --> 00:06:04,178
I'll wait.

87
00:06:12,018 --> 00:06:17,892
Two. Progress starts at "5 of 6". Lines 1
to 4 were unchanged, so Git fast-forwarded

88
00:06:17,932 --> 00:06:21,623
over them, and the first
four commits kept their IDs.

89
00:06:21,963 --> 00:06:27,656
Only the reworded commit and the one after
it are new objects: a2fd360 and fcea3ea.

90
00:06:27,996 --> 00:06:32,234
fixup, to fold "wip" into the
commit above it. Predict again.

91
00:06:40,074 --> 00:06:45,062
Six commits became five, and all five
have new IDs: the first because "wip" was

92
00:06:45,102 --> 00:06:48,625
melded into it, the others
because their parent changed.

93
00:06:48,965 --> 00:06:53,156
On the graph: five new commits,
and the six old ones left behind.

94
00:06:53,496 --> 00:06:57,028
Reordering, to bring "fix test"
directly behind "add test".

95
00:07:00,368 --> 00:07:03,461
One line moved up two
places. No verb changed.

96
00:07:03,801 --> 00:07:06,619
squash, now that the two
test commits are neighbours.

97
00:07:06,659 --> 00:07:10,934
The editor opens on the combined message,
and the scripted editor types a better

98
00:07:10,974 --> 00:07:11,149
one.

99
00:07:14,489 --> 00:07:20,466
What does the editor show at that moment?
labs/run ch09/squash-message, on a cleaner

100
00:07:20,506 --> 00:07:24,191
branch, with cat standing
in for the message editor.

101
00:07:30,531 --> 00:07:35,934
Both messages, each under a comment line:
"This is the 1st commit message", "This is

102
00:07:35,974 --> 00:07:41,377
the commit message #2". Lines that start
with # are removed when you save, so saving

103
00:07:41,417 --> 00:07:45,071
this buffer unchanged gives a
message made of both texts.

104
00:07:45,111 --> 00:07:48,311
fixup skips this step and
keeps the first message.

105
00:07:48,651 --> 00:07:50,240
drop, for the debugging commit.

106
00:07:58,380 --> 00:08:02,867
Three commits, one decision each: a
metric, its test, a second metric.

107
00:08:02,907 --> 00:08:08,234
Each stat shows one file. That's the list
of six from the start of the video, ready

108
00:08:08,274 --> 00:08:09,915
for bisect and for revert.

109
00:08:10,255 --> 00:08:14,586
edit: stop inside the history.
labs/run ch09/interactive-edit.

110
00:08:14,626 --> 00:08:19,733
One commit of this branch mixes a new
metric with a configuration change.

111
00:08:25,873 --> 00:08:30,476
"Stopped at 0527727." The rebase has
replayed that commit and stopped.

112
00:08:30,516 --> 00:08:33,991
You're on a detached HEAD
with the commit as the tip.

113
00:08:34,031 --> 00:08:39,563
To split it, take the commit back while
keeping its changes in the working tree, and

114
00:08:39,603 --> 00:08:41,420
commit them in two portions.

115
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:48,520
git reset HEAD^ 🟡 is a mixed reset: HEAD
and the index go back one commit, the files

116
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:55,239
stay. HEAD~1 names the same commit and is
the safer spelling in shells that treat ^

117
00:08:55,279 --> 00:09:01,796
as a pattern character. Then two commits:
47852a1 for the metric, 6501387 for the

118
00:09:01,836 --> 00:09:02,929
configuration.

119
00:09:03,269 --> 00:09:06,664
Four commits where there were
three. Now the side effect.

120
00:09:06,704 --> 00:09:08,412
Predict what ORIG_HEAD names.

121
00:09:13,252 --> 00:09:17,367
0527727: the mixed commit, not
the tip from before the rebase.

122
00:09:17,407 --> 00:09:22,662
git reset writes ORIG_HEAD too. The reflog
of the branch still has the old tip,

123
00:09:22,702 --> 00:09:28,091
feat/metrics@{1}, and the diff between it
and the new tip prints nothing: the two

124
00:09:28,131 --> 00:09:33,587
trees are identical. That empty diff is
the proof that a split changed history and

125
00:09:33,627 --> 00:09:34,391
not content.

126
00:09:34,731 --> 00:09:39,199
exec: test every commit.
labs/run ch09/interactive-exec.

127
00:09:39,239 --> 00:09:44,432
A rewritten history is a series
of snapshots that nobody has run.

128
00:09:44,472 --> 00:09:47,974
--exec adds an exec line
after every commit.

129
00:09:48,314 --> 00:09:53,952
The check passed after the first commit
and failed after the second: it found a

130
00:09:53,992 --> 00:09:58,553
debug print. Exit status 1. Quick
quiz. Where is the branch now?

131
00:09:58,593 --> 00:10:03,082
A, on the failing commit. B, still
on its old tip. Your answer?

132
00:10:07,922 --> 00:10:11,223
B. HEAD is detached on the
failing commit, 67c1620.

133
00:10:11,263 --> 00:10:15,810
The todo file has the rest of the
list waiting: one pick and one exec.

134
00:10:15,850 --> 00:10:19,480
The branch hasn't moved. Repair
the commit and continue.

135
00:10:19,820 --> 00:10:24,214
git commit --amend 🟡 replaces the failing
commit on the detached HEAD, --continue

136
00:10:24,254 --> 00:10:26,787
runs the rest, and the
check passes at the end.

137
00:10:27,127 --> 00:10:33,678
exec is an ordinary line. With -i --exec
you see the generated lines and may remove

138
00:10:33,718 --> 00:10:39,633
some. labs/run ch09/interactive-exec-lines.
Here only the last one is kept.

139
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:44,471
The check now fails at the tip. You learn
that the branch is broken, not which

140
00:10:44,511 --> 00:10:48,020
commit broke it. That's the
argument for one exec per commit.

141
00:10:48,360 --> 00:10:52,710
break and changing the plan.
labs/run ch09/interactive-break.

142
00:10:52,750 --> 00:10:58,323
break stops the rebase without touching
a commit, so that you can look around.

143
00:10:58,663 --> 00:11:03,731
While any rebase is stopped, you can reopen
the remaining instructions in the editor

144
00:11:03,771 --> 00:11:05,433
with git rebase --edit-todo.

145
00:11:05,773 --> 00:11:10,386
Only the instructions not yet executed
are offered: three lines, not seven.

146
00:11:10,426 --> 00:11:14,108
What is done is done. To change
that, abort and start again.

147
00:11:14,448 --> 00:11:18,644
The first three commits kept their IDs,
the debug commit is gone, and the check

148
00:11:18,684 --> 00:11:19,019
passes.

149
00:11:20,859 --> 00:11:22,507
Five mistakes to watch for.

150
00:11:22,847 --> 00:11:27,619
Deleting a line to "skip it for now".
Root cause: a deleted line is the same as

151
00:11:27,659 --> 00:11:32,309
drop, without a word; rebase.missingCommitsCheck
can turn that silence into a

152
00:11:32,349 --> 00:11:33,528
warning or an error.

153
00:11:33,868 --> 00:11:38,677
Putting squash on the wrong line. Root
cause: squash and fixup meld a commit into

154
00:11:38,717 --> 00:11:42,987
the line above, and the list runs
oldest first, the opposite of git log.

155
00:11:43,327 --> 00:11:46,811
Expecting commits made during
a stop to be on the branch.

156
00:11:46,851 --> 00:11:52,003
Root cause: HEAD is detached during the
whole run and the branch ref moves only when

157
00:11:52,043 --> 00:11:53,116
the list is empty.

158
00:11:53,456 --> 00:11:59,129
Running git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD to undo
an interactive rebase that included a

159
00:11:59,169 --> 00:12:04,557
split. Root cause: the git reset used
for the split overwrote ORIG_HEAD; the

160
00:12:04,597 --> 00:12:07,556
branch's reflog entry @{1} is the old tip.

161
00:12:07,896 --> 00:12:12,043
Keeping one exec at the end "to save time".
Root cause: a failure at the tip says

162
00:12:12,083 --> 00:12:16,230
the branch is broken, not which commit
broke it, and bisect needs every commit to

163
00:12:16,270 --> 00:12:16,540
build.

164
00:12:18,380 --> 00:12:23,978
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation team
has a rule for pull requests that change

165
00:12:24,018 --> 00:12:29,686
metrics code: before the first review, the
author runs git rebase -i --exec "<the

166
00:12:29,726 --> 00:12:31,096
test command>" main.

167
00:12:31,435 --> 00:12:36,366
The reason is the hook of this video, stated
by the textbook: the purpose of a tidy

168
00:12:36,406 --> 00:12:41,336
history isn't beauty. git bisect can find
a regression only if every commit builds,

169
00:12:41,376 --> 00:12:45,708
and a revert removes one decision only
if a commit contains one decision.

170
00:12:45,748 --> 00:12:50,378
A branch cleaned up with one exec per
commit has been proved commit by commit.

171
00:12:50,718 --> 00:12:55,378
The rule costs the author a few minutes.
It has paid for itself the first time a

172
00:12:55,418 --> 00:13:00,254
score regression had to be bisected across
forty commits and every one of them ran.

173
00:13:00,594 --> 00:13:05,791
The team also sets rebase.missingCommitsCheck,
after one engineer lost a commit by

174
00:13:05,831 --> 00:13:10,772
deleting a line. The one line to remember
from the comment block is the one in

175
00:13:10,812 --> 00:13:11,347
capitals.

176
00:13:13,187 --> 00:13:20,178
Your turn. Do Lab 9.1, "Clean up a messy
branch", in lab-manual/m09-rebase.md.

177
00:13:20,518 --> 00:13:21,960
Before you open the editor:

178
00:13:22,300 --> 00:13:23,145
Write the todo list you intend to
save, line by line, oldest first.

179
00:13:23,485 --> 00:13:26,738
Write the git log --oneline
you expect afterwards.

180
00:13:27,078 --> 00:13:29,610
Mark which commits you
expect to keep their IDs.

181
00:13:29,950 --> 00:13:35,181
Then do it in one pass. If the result differs
from your prediction, find the old tip

182
00:13:35,221 --> 00:13:38,381
in the branch's reflog, go
back, and predict again.

183
00:13:38,721 --> 00:13:45,741
The challenge is Exercise 9.7, Level 3, "a
rebase that stopped at an exec line", in

184
00:13:45,781 --> 00:13:48,548
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

185
00:13:50,388 --> 00:13:55,299
Q403: "During a rebase, when does the
branch ref move, and what follows from that

186
00:13:55,339 --> 00:13:58,110
for commits made while
the rebase is stopped?"

187
00:14:00,450 --> 00:14:05,488
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
gives the moment precisely, and says where

188
00:14:05,528 --> 00:14:10,565
HEAD is in the meantime. Then it draws
consequences, at least three: for a commit

189
00:14:10,605 --> 00:14:15,831
made during a stop, for what --abort returns
to, and for which names still reach the

190
00:14:15,871 --> 00:14:21,034
old tip afterwards. If you mention a command
that can be run during a stop and that

191
00:14:21,074 --> 00:14:25,735
changes one of those names, you have
shown that you have done this by hand.

192
00:14:27,575 --> 00:14:30,503
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

193
00:14:30,843 --> 00:14:35,944
An interactive rebase hands me the todo
list before running it, and the list runs

194
00:14:35,984 --> 00:14:37,722
top to bottom, oldest first.

195
00:14:38,062 --> 00:14:42,854
reword changes a message, fixup and squash
meld a commit into the line above, a

196
00:14:42,894 --> 00:14:47,991
moved line replays the commit at its new
position, and drop or a deleted line leaves

197
00:14:48,031 --> 00:14:48,419
it out.

198
00:14:48,759 --> 00:14:53,354
edit stops after a commit so that I can
amend it or split it with a mixed reset and

199
00:14:53,394 --> 00:14:54,247
several commits.

200
00:14:54,587 --> 00:14:59,455
exec runs a command after a commit and
stops on a non-zero status, and break stops

201
00:14:59,495 --> 00:15:00,831
without doing anything.

202
00:15:01,171 --> 00:15:06,058
During every stop HEAD is detached and the
branch hasn't moved, and after a split I

203
00:15:06,098 --> 00:15:09,738
take the old tip from the branch's
reflog, not from ORIG_HEAD.

204
00:15:11,578 --> 00:15:14,144
Read section 9.6 of Chapter 9.

205
00:15:14,484 --> 00:15:21,071
Repeat Lab 9.1, "Clean up a messy branch",
in lab-manual/m09-rebase.md until the

206
00:15:21,111 --> 00:15:22,894
result takes one pass.

207
00:15:23,234 --> 00:15:28,013
You turned six messy commits into three
clean ones today, and proved each one with a

208
00:15:28,053 --> 00:15:31,112
test. Practise it in the lab
until one pass is enough.

209
